what food do you love I love my mom's  chicken tacos simple chicken and a  tortilla but you come to that dining  table and she has a spread of guacamole  and homemade salsa fresh tortillas pico  de gallo queso fresco and you were  bumping elbows with people just trying  to fill your taco it's a mess but  there's laughter and conversation flows  and every once in a while you can catch  mom leaning back  silent smiling because her family is  happy and fed and loved  that's what food is food is love and  today I want to remind you of that as  business leaders but as consumers I also  want to make you aware that food is  business my mom cooked dinner every  night and though I may not have liked  every meal at that table I never  questioned whether it was good for me my  mom loved me and the food she fed me  with a gesture of care taking from  infancy all of our relationships with  food are most influenced by those who  first feed and nurture us and over the  course of our lifetime were socialized  to associate trust with food but can you  trust every hand that feeds you for me  it would take a personal health crisis  before I discovered that no you cannot  that once I leave my mom's dinner table  food becomes a product and I have to  evaluate it like I would any other  purchase as I got older mom I went out  with friends but snacks at the grocery  store at the gas station at school and  yet I never stopped to question what was  in the food I was eating until my senior  year of high school when my mom showed  me my graduation photos I had gained so  much weight  I couldn't recognize my face my doctor  checked my BMI I was clinically  overweight I was one stage away from  obesity I felt helpless I didn't know  what to do I didn't know what to eat so  my freshman year of college I kept a  food diary and I started exercising  daily and I took my first nutrition  class and I learned that from calories  to chemicals what's in the food that I  eat is something I have to know before I  can make a healthy choice that  experience was 10 years and 25 pounds  ago and it's what inspired me to become  a dietitian and whether working to serve  kids in a school cafeteria or to  implement national nutrition policy in  the White House I take that experience  with me the simple revelation that the  majority of the food that I need is not  the majority of the food being sold to  me the grocery store does not have an  investment in my health and well-being  the way my mom did the grocery store is  a business products line shelves and at  the eye level of children they're meant  to reduce what's called the nav effect  kids baking mom and dad to buy that  sugary cereal and a year eye level boxes  covered with blues and whites and greens  and new feelings of peacefulness  tranquility safety making you think that  that bag is wholesome a healthy choice  and just as you've survived that grocery  store experience you get to the checkout  aisle welcomed by magazines claiming to  know the next great superfood or  condemning the next thing you should  never eat again these tactics frustrate  all of us in answering a simple question  what should I eat and as it turns out 82  percent of Americans are confused about  that question our struggle to eat well  has serious implications  one in three adults are overweight or  obese one in two children in this  generation will have diabetes at some  point in their lifetimes and diet has  surpassed smoking as the number one  predictor of death and disease in this  country the confusion reaches us here in  this room I've had conversations with  many of you dispelling the  misinformation about food  paleo sugar-free gluten-free all  reasonable questions in a landscape of  misinformation but today I want to give  you a more important insight a way of  thinking about food that I think can  frame any choice that you ever make  before a purchase and it's this we are  programmed to oversimplify the  complexity of food your brain constantly  seeks to conserve energy by eliminating  the challenge of difficult choices we  are especially acclaimed to do this with  regard to products that we want to trust  like food we choose easy mental routes  instead of complicated ones so that we  can defer our mental energy for more  difficult challenges for example we  choose fast food on our way home from  the grocery store we believe those  magazine covers or there's commercials  timed at dinner right when you're hungry  and order in we go every afternoon for  an afternoon pick-me-up grabbing a soda  when we repeat these behaviors your  brain forms a feedback loop that  eventually needs no decision making  effort at all in order to create that  behavior and what happens is your brain  goes from making active decisions to  forming habits now habits are 45% of the  behaviors you make every day and they  maintain your productivity they reduce  your mental exhaustion but in the realm  of food they jeopardize your health  they induced you to believe those  nutrition claims those products that are  so conveniently located with just the  right message at just the right time  begging to do your thinking for you do  you know the difference between a claim  and the truth turns out it could save  your health conscious eaters not only  make healthy decisions despite increases  in price and decreases inconvenience  they have significantly less incidence  of diabetes heart disease and cancer so  how do you become a conscious eater  it starts with committing to educate  yourself on what's in your food you got  to learn more to make the right choice  and you'll start to find meaningful  terms on the front of packages certified  and regulate regulated labels like  organic and you'll start to learn that  the word natural has no meaning you may  find that most differing in your  nutrition needs are met from fruits and  vegetables lean proteins water products  that you find on the perimeter of the  grocery store you may start shopping  less in the inner aisles and as you buy  those fresh foods you may be enticed to  cook them and you'll find that just a  couple of simple tricks like sauteing or  steaming open up a whole new world of  food choices you will become conscious  of your decisions you'll know what's in  your food you'll know whether it's the  right amount and you'll know where it's  from this is my hope my aspiration for  the nation that if each one of us makes  one more conscious choice our collective  demand will generate systemic change  across our entire food environment and  that our farmers and our supply chain  managers our manufacturers and our  grocers will respond with more  accessible  more affordable nutritious choices for  everyone because food is a business and  as consumers we have the power to demand  what we want with what we purchase and  as business leaders we have the power to  take advantage of this opportunity  healthy demand is already increasing 82%  of consumers want to see healthier  products from companies and 70% of CPG  sales in recent years came from offering  those healthy options we have the  opportunity to meet that demand we have  the opportunity to make the fact that  food is love and food is business a  shared value because that's who we are  we're business leaders and we're  consumers and we'll be making decisions  about the food we eat for ourselves and  for our loved ones the rest of our lives  and our choices today will shape the  food environment of tomorrow so today I  hope you remember as business leaders  that food is love and as consumers I  hope you remember the food is business  and most of all I hope the next choice  you make is a conscious one trust me  it's good for you  you
 

